I would replace that whole paragraph with this:

The model's local prediction confidence is estimated from the uncertainty of the chunk-level emotion distribution. First, the chunk logits are converted into class probabilities:
\[
\mathbf{p}_i^{(m)}
=
\operatorname{softmax}\left(\mathbf{\ell}_i^{(m)}\right).
\]

The predictive uncertainty of the \(m\)-th chunk is then quantified using Shannon entropy:
\[
H_i^{(m)}
=
-\sum_{c=1}^{C}
p_{i,c}^{(m)}
\log\left(p_{i,c}^{(m)}+\epsilon\right),
\]
where \(C\) denotes the number of emotion classes and \(\epsilon>0\) is a small constant introduced for numerical stability.

An inverse-entropy confidence score is subsequently defined as:
\[
\widetilde{c}_i^{(m)}
=
\frac{1}{H_i^{(m)}+\epsilon}.
\]

Thus, chunks exhibiting a concentrated class-probability distribution receive higher confidence scores, whereas chunks with more uncertain predictions receive lower confidence. To stabilize the relative scale of the confidence values during optimization, the scores are normalized by the mean confidence of the processed chunk pool and constrained to a bounded range:
\[
c_i^{(m)}
=
\operatorname{clip}
\left(
\frac{\widetilde{c}_i^{(m)}}
{\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\widetilde{c}_j},
\,c_{\min},\,c_{\max}
\right),
\]
where \(K\) denotes the total number of chunks in the mini-batch, and \(c_{\min}=0.1\) and \(c_{\max}=10.0\) correspond to the bounds used in the implementation.

The final chunk importance weight is then obtained by combining the learned emotional saliency with the normalized predictive confidence:
\[
\gamma_i^{(m)}
=
\alpha_i^{(m)}c_i^{(m)}.
\]

This now matches the implementation much more closely. The code computes softmax probabilities, entropy, inverse entropy, mean normalization, and clipping before using the resulting confidence in the \(\alpha\times c\) audio-logit weighting. Pasted text

